  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nToday, near-surface conditions are still on the drier side in \nspite of moisture creeping into the region. Today's 18Z sounding\nindicates an environment similar to this time yesterday, albeit\nwith less instability. Like yesterday, storm development is \nexpected, though virga showers will likely dominate with sparse \nareas of measurable rain (primarily over the high terrain) and \nlittle chance for wetting rain. On Wednesday, the broad high \ncentered overhead skirts eastwards, shifting upper-level flow \nfrom westerly to southerly/southwesterly. In this shift, mild \nmoisture from the Gulf and the Baja Peninsula rises northwards \nthrough the Desert Southwest towards our region. More notably \nthough is the potent moisture plume to our west, which is \ncurrently rising into the Great Basin. The shifting high will \npull that moisture eastwards over our CWA, which should be the \nfinal push needed to saturate our low-levels and bring about \nanother round of widespread showers and thunderstorms.\n\nThe strongest portions of the moisture plume slide north of us \nthrough Wyoming, but ensemble PWAT's are forecasted at 150-180 \npercent of normal across portions of the CWA nonetheless. This \nshould be sufficient to prompt widespread rainfall Wednesday \nonwards, including some stronger cells capable of producing \nlocalized heavy rainfall. The threat for flash flooding and \ndebris flows appears to be maximized on Thursday, but a marginal\nthreat remains in place Wednesday, Friday, and possibly through\nthe weekend as well. The manifestation of this threat will \ndepend on when and where we see substantial lift and/or \ninstability. The lift part of that equation is where uncertainty\nis greatest. Deterministic models are hinting at some shortwave\nfeatures transiting the ridge through the mid- and late-week \nperiod, though there is very little agreement on when these will\noccur, if they manifest at all. Models are better-aligned in \nregards to instability though. Per yesterday's 00Z sounding, \nmixed-layer CAPE neared 1000 J/kg, and deterministic models are \nsignaling that this sort of instability, if not stronger, will \ncontinue through the work week. This will also mean potential \nfor sub-severe hail and winds. As the low-level atmosphere \ncontinues to moisten up today and Wednesday, gusty winds will be\nfavored.\n\nIn spite of the model agreement, some doubt in the development \nof strong instability is being cast due to the anticipated \nscattered cloud cover during mornings. This is often one of the \nbiggest caveats we must consider during monsoonal pushes, and it\nwill be a key consideration over the coming days. Should \nmorning cloud cover be ample, look for thunderstorms to \ngenerally be short-lived and more stratiform-type storms to \nquickly develop. Conversely, if clearer morning skies present \nthemselves, stronger discrete storms will be favored. At this \ntime, it is too difficult to speculate which mode will come to \nfruition on which days, so stay up-to-date with future forecast \nupdates for additional information. Either way, wet weather is \non its way.",
